.TH "FEROPROBE-DIFF" "1" "" "FeroProbe 0.3.0" "FeroProbe Reference Manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
feroprobe-diff - compare two FeroProbe or nmap scan reports
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
feroprobe-diff [options] FILE1 FILE2
\f[R]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[V]FILE1\f[R] is the earlier scan and \f[V]FILE2\f[R] the later one;
both must be XML reports (\f[V]-oX\f[R], or the \f[V].xml\f[R] file of
\f[V]-oA\f[R]).
Like the standard \f[V]diff\f[R], \f[V]feroprobe-diff\f[R] compares two
scans at a time and by default shows only what differs.
Things it compares:
.IP \[bu] 2
Host states (up, down) and which hosts are in each scan
.IP \[bu] 2
Host addresses and names
.IP \[bu] 2
Port states (open, closed, filtered, ...), including the \[dq]Not
shown\[dq] counts
.IP \[bu] 2
Services and versions (from \f[V]-sV\f[R]), including the SSL/TLS tunnel
.IP \[bu] 2
OS matches (from \f[V]-O\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
Script output (pre-scan, host and port scripts), for nmap reports and
for FeroProbe\[aq]s once it has scripts
.PP
The command lines and times of the two scans are shown when they differ,
but do not count as a difference for the exit status.
.PP
A file written with \f[V]--append-output\f[R] holds one XML document per
run and cannot be read as one scan; split it into one file per run
first.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS \f[V]-v\f[R], \f[V]--verbose\f[R]
.PP
Show every host and every port, not only the ones that changed.
Unchanged lines start with a space.
.SS \f[V]--text\f[R]
.PP
Write the differences as text (the default).
See Text output.
.SS \f[V]--xml\f[R]
.PP
Write the differences as XML.
See XML output.
\f[V]--text\f[R] and \f[V]--xml\f[R] cannot be combined.
.SS \f[V]-h\f[R], \f[V]--help\f[R]
.PP
Print a short help and exit.
.SS \f[V]-V\f[R], \f[V]--version\f[R]
.PP
Print the version and exit.
.SH TEXT OUTPUT
.PP
The text output looks like a unified diff of the normal scan output.
Each line starts with a mark:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]-\f[R]: the line is in the first scan only
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]+\f[R]: the line is in the second scan only
.IP \[bu] 2
a space: the line is in both (shown for context, and for everything with
\f[V]-v\f[R])
.PP
A line that changed is a \f[V]-\f[R] line followed by a \f[V]+\f[R]
line.
Each host that changed gets a block, after a blank line:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
-FeroProbe 0.5.0 scan initiated Sun Nov 16 08:00:00 2025 UTC as: feroprobe -sV -O 10.0.0.0/28
+FeroProbe 0.5.0 scan initiated Mon Nov 17 08:00:00 2025 UTC as: feroprobe -sV -O 10.0.0.0/28

 web.lan (10.0.0.5):
 PORT     STATE SERVICE    VERSION
-22/tcp   open  ssh        OpenSSH 9.6p1 (protocol 2.0)
-80/tcp   open  http
+80/tcp   open  http       nginx 1.24.0
-443/tcp  open  http
+443/tcp  open  ssl/http
+8080/tcp open  http-proxy
 OS details:
-  Linux 4.19 - 6.x
+  Linux 3.x - 4.18

-10.0.0.10:
-Host is up.
-Not shown: 1000 filtered ports

+10.0.0.11:
+Host is up.
+Not shown: 999 closed ports
+PORT    STATE SERVICE VERSION
+161/udp open  snmp

-old-name.lan (10.0.0.12):
+new-name.lan (10.0.0.12):
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Here port 22 on 10.0.0.5 is no longer open (it fell into the second
scan\[aq]s \[dq]Not shown\[dq] ports), port 80 gained a version, port
443 now speaks TLS, port 8080 opened and the OS match changed.
10.0.0.10 was not in the second scan, 10.0.0.11 was not in the first,
and 10.0.0.12 got a new reverse DNS name.
.PP
A host\[aq]s block has these parts, each only when it changed (or with
\f[V]-v\f[R]): the host\[aq]s name line, \f[V]Host is <state>.\f[R],
\f[V]Not shown: <counts>\f[R], the port table with the changed ports,
\f[V]OS details:\f[R] with the OS matches, and
\f[V]Host script results:\f[R].
Script output is shown under its port, in the \f[V]|  id: output\f[R] /
\f[V]|_ last line\f[R] layout of the normal output.
Pre-scan and post-scan script results come before and after the hosts.
.SH XML OUTPUT
.PP
\f[V]--xml\f[R] writes a document in ndiff\[aq]s layout, so tools that
read ndiff\[aq]s XML read it too.
The root element is \f[V]<nmapdiff version=\[dq]1\[dq]>\f[R], holding
one \f[V]<scandiff>\f[R].
Inside it the scan report\[aq]s own elements are used
(\f[V]<nmaprun>\f[R], \f[V]<host>\f[R], \f[V]<status>\f[R],
\f[V]<address>\f[R], \f[V]<hostnames>\f[R], \f[V]<ports>\f[R],
\f[V]<extraports>\f[R], \f[V]<port>\f[R], \f[V]<state>\f[R],
\f[V]<service>\f[R], \f[V]<os>\f[R], \f[V]<osmatch>\f[R],
\f[V]<script>\f[R], \f[V]<prescript>\f[R], \f[V]<postscript>\f[R],
\f[V]<hostscript>\f[R]), with these additions:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<hostdiff>\f[R] around each host that changed, and
\f[V]<portdiff>\f[R] around each port that changed.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]<a>\f[R] around what is in the first scan only, \f[V]<b>\f[R]
around what is in the second scan only.
A host in one scan only is a \f[V]<hostdiff>\f[R] holding the whole
\f[V]<host>\f[R] in \f[V]<a>\f[R] or \f[V]<b>\f[R].
A port whose state changed is a \f[V]<portdiff>\f[R] with the whole
\f[V]<port>\f[R] in \f[V]<a>\f[R] and in \f[V]<b>\f[R]; a port whose
state stayed but whose service changed has the two \f[V]<service>\f[R]
elements in \f[V]<a>\f[R] and \f[V]<b>\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
When the runs differ (scanner, version, command line, start or end
time), the two \f[V]<nmaprun>\f[R] elements in \f[V]<a>\f[R] and
\f[V]<b>\f[R] come first.
.PP
Elements carry only the attributes that are compared:
\f[V]<service>\f[R] has \f[V]name\f[R], \f[V]product\f[R],
\f[V]version\f[R], \f[V]extrainfo\f[R] and \f[V]tunnel\f[R];
\f[V]<osmatch>\f[R] has \f[V]name\f[R].
.SH HOW SCANS ARE COMPARED
.PP
\f[B]Hosts\f[R] are paired by address: a host in both scans is the one
with the same first address (IPv4 before IPv6 before MAC), or the same
name for a host without an address.
A host whose addresses or names changed is shown with its old name line
(\f[V]-\f[R]) and its new one (\f[V]+\f[R]).
Hosts are listed in address order.
.PP
\f[B]Ports\f[R] are paired by number and protocol.
A port that a scan does not list (because it is in that scan\[aq]s
\[dq]Not shown\[dq] group, or was not scanned) counts as not in that
scan: it gets a \f[V]-\f[R] line only, or a \f[V]+\f[R] line only.
A port that both scans fold into \[dq]Not shown\[dq] is not compared on
its own; the \[dq]Not shown\[dq] counts cover it.
.PP
\f[B]Services\f[R] are compared on their name, product, version, extra
information and SSL/TLS tunnel.
Other details of the report (reasons, round-trip times, CPEs,
confidence) are not compared.
.PP
\f[B]OS matches\f[R] are compared as a list, best match first: added and
removed matches are marked, unchanged ones in between are shown for
context.
.PP
\f[B]Script results\f[R] are paired by script id; their output is
compared line by line.
Very long outputs that differ in many scattered places (beyond about
four million line pairs to align) are shown as the old block replaced by
the new one.
.SH DIFFERENCES FROM NDIFF
.PP
\f[V]feroprobe-diff\f[R] follows ndiff, except where ndiff\[aq]s
behaviour is clearly wrong:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
T{
Behaviour
T}@T{
ndiff
T}@T{
feroprobe-diff
T}
_
T{
Pairing hosts
T}@T{
By first address and first host name together, so a host whose reverse
DNS name changed shows as one host gone and another new (ndiff\[aq]s own
manual still shows it as one host with a new name)
T}@T{
By address alone: the host shows once, with its old and new name
T}
T{
Host order
T}@T{
Addresses sorted as text (10.0.0.10 before 10.0.0.2)
T}@T{
Numeric address order
T}
T{
SSL/TLS tunnel
T}@T{
A service that gains or loses its tunnel (\f[V]http\f[R] to
\f[V]ssl/http\f[R]) is not counted as a change
T}@T{
Counted and shown
T}
T{
Pre- and post-scan script changes
T}@T{
Shown, but not counted for the exit status
T}@T{
Counted
T}
T{
Times
T}@T{
Local time
T}@T{
UTC, like the rest of FeroProbe\[aq]s output
T}
T{
Scanner name in the banner
T}@T{
\f[V]Nmap\f[R], or the report\[aq]s \f[V]scanner\f[R] attribute
T}@T{
The same, with FeroProbe\[aq]s reports shown as \f[V]FeroProbe\f[R]
T}
T{
Version option
T}@T{
None
T}@T{
\f[V]-V\f[R], \f[V]--version\f[R]
T}
.TE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]0\f[R]: the scans are the same in everything compared
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]1\f[R]: the scans differ
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]2\f[R]: an error, such as a file that cannot be opened or read, or
a wrong option
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Compare yesterday\[aq]s scan with today\[aq]s:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
feroprobe-diff scan-2026-11-15.xml scan-2026-11-16.xml
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Scan a network every day and mail the changes, from cron:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
#!/bin/sh
date=$(date +%F)
cd /var/lib/scans
# A failed scan leaves the last good report as the baseline.
feroprobe -T4 -F -sV 10.0.0.0/24 -oA scan-$date > /dev/null || exit 1
if [ -e scan-prev.xml ]; then
    status=0
    feroprobe-diff scan-prev.xml scan-$date.xml > diff-$date || status=$?
    # 0 same, 1 changed; anything else (2: a report could not be read)
    # keeps the baseline and stops.
    [ $status -gt 1 ] && exit $status
    [ $status -eq 1 ] && mail -s \[dq]Network changes $date\[dq] admin < diff-$date
fi
ln -sf scan-$date.xml scan-prev.xml
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Show everything, changed or not, as XML:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
feroprobe-diff -v --xml before.xml after.xml
\f[R]
.fi
